    "media:imagetext": "Mr. Bixby\n- 3-\nSeptember 22, 1937\nthat nations manage to get small arm ammnition somehow\nor other in spite\nof the most rigorous restrictions, and with this type of supplies alone the\nChinese should be able to keep up a devastating guerilla warfare for a\nvery long period. After all, they have only one chance of winning, or\non a daris theme ree,\nshall we say saving themselves, in this war and that is to bleed Japan eco-\nnomically and financially white by forcing her to keep a very large army\nmobile in the field for a long period. If they do, something is going to\ncrack badly in Japan, I think,\nbefore anything cracks in China. It is for\nthis reason that I cannot believe in any return to peaceful conditions in\nthe future as far ahead as I can see--which means, of course, that in my\nopinion there is not the slightest chance of our resuming operations or\nparticipating in operations in China even if we were disposed to make the\nnecessary further investment.\nI have felt all along that the possibility of our operating\na purely commercial service was illusory, not so much because of the fail-\nure of the Chinese to cooperate honestly\n(since I did think the \"top-side\"\nmen would do that in their own interests) but because I felt sure that the\nin China so long as Japanese military aircraft are active anywhere in the\nterritory, even although a state of comparative peace might materialize and\nour Chinese friends should give new and possibly more satisfactory assur-\nances of their willingness to cooperate.\nPossibly you will disagree with a good deal of the above and\nit would be interesting to have your comment,\nas I make no claims to omni-\nIn any case, correspondence of this sort is interesting and stimu-\nlating, like a game of chess by cable.\nSincerely yours,\nSVM: AH",
